    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's focus on the technical settings, shall we? When it comes to polyester fabric, a good starting point for temperature settings is between 300°F to 350°F (150°C to 175°C). However, this can vary depending on the specific polyester blend and the desired color intensity.\n\nAs for pressure, a good rule of thumb is to start with a medium to high pressure setting, around 10-15 pounds per square inch (PSI). This will help ensure that the heat press is applying enough pressure to transfer the colors evenly. But, be careful not to overdo it, or you may end up with a fabric that's pucked or distorted.\n\nIt's also worth noting that the type of heat press you're using can affect the results. Are you using a manual or automatic heat press? The automatic settings may be more forgiving, but the manual settings can give you more control over the temperature and pressure.",
